AUDIO: 'system called Aspire. If you\'ve been using AI agents like codecs or clawed code, you should know that you can create custom skills which the agent can reuse in the future. For example, if you wanted to carry out a specific workflow.'

[22:47] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Well, what happens if we applied these skills to robotics? Well, that\'s exactly what Aspire does. So, this is a self-improving system for robotics. It lets robots learn from their own failed attempts and then turn those lessons'

[23:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'into reusable skills. Normally, robot learning can be painfully repetitive. A robot tries something, fails, and unless the system is designed well, that failure does not automatically become useful knowledge for the future. But'

[23:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Aspire changes that loop. It writes robot control programs as code, runs them, watches what happens, and if something goes wrong, it will repair the code and validate the corrected behavior, then save it into a skill'

[23:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'library so that it remembers not to commit this mistake again the next time. Now, this system has three major pieces. It has a closed loop execution engine, a growing skill library, and an'

[23:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'evolutionary search procedure that helps it explore better and better code to use. And this is essentially a self-improving system. It\'s not just training one policy for one task, but building a system that can keep'

