VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'yourself. If you\'re interested in reading further, I\'ll link to this main page in the description below. Also this week we have a really powerful way to generate images extremely quickly and this is called RDM which stands for'

[31:43] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'representation distribution matching. You see most modern image models are diffusion models. They generate images by first taking a canvas of random gausian noise and then at each step it removes some of that noise until you get'

[31:56] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'your final image. Now usually full models require about 30 to 50 steps. We also have faster flash or turbo models which require only like four to 10 steps. But even four to 10 steps still requires some time to generate. What if'

[32:10] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'you could generate an image in just one step? Well, that\'s exactly what this project is about. This can generate an image in just one step and the quality is not bad. So, here are some example images for your reference. it is still'

[32:23] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'kind of lacking in detail and there are some minor errors and that\'s because it\'s only just one step and the quality does seem to be better than other one-step models. They use something called representation distribution'

[32:35] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'matching or RDM which is quite technical but it basically teaches the AI to match the overall distribution of high-level visual features. So rather than memorizing one photo at a time, think of it as learning the general style and'

[32:48] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'characteristics that real photos tend to have. The nice thing is at the top they released the code to this already. So if you click on this code button and you scroll down a bit here, it contains all the instructions on how to run this locally on your computer. If you\'re'

